# The ICER, the plane, and why net benefit is easier to think with
next: {"title":"Where does the threshold come from - willingness to pay, or what we displace?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/where-does-the-threshold-come-from.cast"}

## One picture, two drugs
voice: male
level: advanced
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: One picture, two drugs
    x_label: Incremental effect ΔE (QALYs)
    y_label: Incremental cost ΔC (million kr)
    wtp_threshold: 0.5
    quadrant_labels: false
    points:
      - {label: Current care, effect: 0, cost: 0}
      - {label: Nusinersen, effect: 3, cost: 6, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1.5, cost: 0.12}

    arrow arrow_nus pt_0 -> pt_1 #b5482e hidden true
    arrow arrow_hip pt_0 -> pt_2 #2f6b8f hidden true
    label slope_note "λ = 500 000 kr per QALY" above-left #8f887c attach_to wtp_line hidden true

Over the next five parts we'll read the cost-effectiveness plane, watch the ICER misbehave, and replace it with net benefit — starting today with two Norwegian decisions on one picture.
    draw x_axis y_axis

Everything here is incremental, so current care sits at the origin: zero extra cost, zero extra QALYs, by construction.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

    pause 0.3

Nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy: with rounded teaching numbers, about three extra QALYs per patient for roughly six million kroner more.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

    pause 0.3

And the unglamorous comparison: a hip replacement, about one and a half QALYs for a hundred and twenty thousand kroner — it barely lifts off the horizontal axis.
    draw pt_2 pt_label_2

Zoom in and it is a real point with real people in it; it only looks like nothing beside six million.
    camera center.ref pt_2 zoom 2.2

    camera reset true duration 1

Now the move that makes the whole picture readable: draw the arrow from the origin out to a point, and the ICER is just its slope — extra cost over extra effect.
    draw arrow_nus

Six million kroner divided by three QALYs: two million kroner per quality-adjusted life year.
    point at.ref pt_1 gesture tap

    pause 0.3

The hip arrow is almost flat: a hundred and twenty thousand over one and a half QALYs, about eighty thousand kroner each.
    draw arrow_hip

    quiz "What does the steepness of the arrow from the origin to a point measure?" intro "A quick check."
        right: The slope is ΔC divided by ΔE — the ICER itself, in kroner per QALY.
        wrong: Read the arrow as a rise over a run: cost over effect.
        + The ICER
        * The net monetary benefit
        * The number of patients treated

The threshold is a slope as well — a line through the origin at, for illustration, half a million kroner per QALY.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label slope_note

    quiz "Which point lies above the threshold line?"
        right: Nusinersen's slope is roughly two million per QALY — about four times steeper than the line.
        wrong: Compare each arrow's steepness with the line's, not the height of the point alone.
        + Nusinersen
        * Hip replacement
        * Neither

Below the line, hips buy health more cheaply than we have said health is worth.
    highlight pt_2 effect glow

Above it sits nusinersen — which is why Beslutningsforum first said no, and then yes only for patients under eighteen.
    highlight pt_1 effect glow

Notice how little arithmetic we actually did: two slopes, compared against one slope.
    erase arrow_nus arrow_hip

That single number, lambda, is what turns a picture of costs and effects into a decision.
    mark mark target slope_note kind box

But this tidy geometry rested on both arrows pointing up and to the right — a ratio of two positive numbers. Move a point into another quadrant and the same ratio starts telling lies.
    delivery grave

One picture, two drugs: the ICER is nothing but the steepness of an arrow, and the threshold is the steepness we have agreed to accept.
    draw title

## The Four Quadrants
voice: male
level: advanced
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: The four quadrants of the plane
    x_label: ΔE — incremental QALYs
    y_label: ΔC — incremental cost (1000 kr)
    wtp_threshold: 275
    quadrant_labels: true
    points:
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1.5, cost: 120, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Costly new drug, effect: 0.6, cost: 400}
      - {label: Older branded option, effect: -0.4, cost: 150}
      - {label: Generic switch, effect: 0.25, cost: -60}
      - {label: Withdraw low-value service, effect: -0.35, cost: -250}

Now that we have seen two drugs land on the same picture, let's read the whole map — because only half of it can be decided without naming a price for health.
    draw x_axis y_axis parallel true

Start south-east, the comfortable corner: moving a patient group onto a generic saves about 60 000 kroner each and gains a fraction of a QALY — cheaper and better, what we call dominant.
    draw q_se pt_3 pt_label_3

    pause 0.3

North-west is its mirror: an older branded option costing 150 000 more and delivering 0.4 QALYs less — dominated, and rejecting it needs no threshold at all.
    draw q_nw pt_2 pt_label_2

    quiz "Which cases can be decided without naming any threshold?"
        intro: A quick check before we go on.
        right: Dominant and dominated cases settle themselves — better and cheaper, or worse and dearer, needs no price of health.
        wrong: Look again at south-east and north-west: those two are settled by dominance alone.
        * The north-east point
        + The dominant and dominated points
        * Only the south-west point

North-east is where almost everything real lives: a hip replacement costs about 120 000 kroner more and buys roughly 1.5 extra QALYs.
    draw q_ne pt_0 pt_label_0

To judge it you must commit to a price of health — lambda, drawn as a line through the origin, here 275 000 kroner per QALY.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

The hip sits below that line: the health it buys is worth more than the resources it consumes.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

    pause 0.4

A costlier drug lands above the line — it buys real health too, it simply asks more per QALY than we have said we will pay; nusinersen, from the last part, sits far above the top of this frame.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

Three quadrants down. The fourth is the one committees quietly hate.
    erase q_se q_nw q_ne

South-west: withdrawing a low-value service releases about 250 000 kroner per patient and costs roughly 0.35 QALYs — you are giving up health to free resources.
    draw q_sw pt_4 pt_label_4

The same rule holds, and it inverts your intuition — below the line here means the money released is worth more than the health surrendered, so this disinvestment is acceptable.
    focus pt_4 wtp_line

    quiz "A disinvestment sits below the threshold line in the south-west. What does that mean?" intro "One more."
        right: Below the line in the south-west means the resources freed outweigh the QALYs given up — acceptable, if uncomfortable.
        wrong: It cannot be dominant or dominated: health falls, so the trade-off is against the money released.
        + It releases more money than the lost health is worth
        * It is dominated
        * It saves money and adds health

Yet a system like Beslutningsforum almost never signs a decision in this corner: it can say no to a new drug, while low-value care already inside the budget is displaced silently, through queues rather than minutes.
    mark sw_mark target pt_label_4 kind box

    pause 0.4

Two quadrants decide themselves; the other two only decide once you name a price for a QALY — and in the south-west, naming it means selling health for money.
    draw title delivery grave

## Why the ICER Lies
voice: male
level: advanced
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    title: Why the ICER lies
    x_label: ΔE (QALYs)
    y_label: ΔC (kr)
    quadrant_labels: true
    points:
      - {label: 'A: +2 QALYs, −400 000 kr', effect: 2, cost: -400000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: 'B: −2 QALYs, +400 000 kr', effect: -2, cost: 400000, emphasize: true}
      - {label: 'Trial 1: ΔE = +0.02', effect: 0.02, cost: 500000}
      - {label: 'Trial 2: ΔE = −0.02', effect: -0.02, cost: 500000}
      - {label: 'X: +1 QALY, +300 000 kr', effect: 1, cost: 300000}
      - {label: 'Y: +2 QALYs, +500 000 kr', effect: 2, cost: 500000}

Now that we have read the four quadrants, let's put the ICER itself under suspicion — because a committee that ranks technologies by delta C over delta E can approve the wrong one.
    draw x_axis y_axis

    draw q_nw q_ne q_sw q_se parallel true

Option A saves four hundred thousand kroner and gains two QALYs: cheaper and better, sitting in the southeast — it dominates current care.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

    pause 0.3

Option B costs four hundred thousand more and loses two QALYs: the northwest, dominated, everything you never want to buy.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

    pause 0.3

Now divide. Minus four hundred thousand over plus two; plus four hundred thousand over minus two. Both come out as minus two hundred thousand kroner per QALY.
    text calc1 "Both: ΔC / ΔE = −200 000 kr per QALY" #b5482e x 500 y 45 font_size 26

The best result in the whole plane and the worst carry the same ICER, because a ratio keeps the signs only as their product.
    highlight pt_0 pt_1 effect glow delivery grave

    pause 0.5

Which is why the reporting guidelines of most agencies forbid printing a negative ICER at all — you write the word dominant, or dominated, and let the number go.
    clear keep ["x_axis","y_axis","q_nw","q_ne","q_sw","q_se"]

The second failure lives where delta E is almost nothing: two analyses of the same drug, a gain of two hundredths of a QALY and a loss of the same size, half a million kroner either way.
    draw pt_2 pt_label_2 pt_3 pt_label_3 parallel true

To the eye they are one dot — about a week of healthy life, far inside any confidence interval.
    camera center.ref pt_2 zoom 2.4

But the ratio reads plus twenty-five million per QALY, then minus twenty-five million: the denominator crossing zero throws the number out to infinity and back.
    text calc2 "+25 000 000   and   −25 000 000 kr per QALY" #b5482e x 500 y 45 font_size 26

So an ICER has no honest confidence interval — it blows up precisely where the effect is uncertain, which is precisely where a decision-maker needs help.
    camera reset true

    pause 0.4

Third failure: ICERs cannot be lined up against each other.
    clear keep ["x_axis","y_axis","q_nw","q_ne","q_sw","q_se"]

Treatment X against current care buys one QALY for three hundred thousand kroner — an ICER of three hundred thousand.
    draw pt_4 pt_label_4

Treatment Y buys two QALYs for five hundred thousand — two hundred and fifty thousand per QALY, so by the league table Y is the better buy.
    draw pt_5 pt_label_5

Yet if X is already on the table, moving from X to Y costs two hundred thousand more for one more QALY — a third number, and the only one that answers the choice actually in front of you.
    text calc3 "Y vs X: 200 000 / 1 = 200 000 kr per QALY" #b5482e x 500 y 45 font_size 26

Every ICER belongs to a pair of options, never to one, so a column of ICERs against placebo is arithmetic without meaning.
    highlight pt_4 pt_5 effect pulse

    quiz "An ICER of −400 000 kr per QALY — which quadrant is that technology in?" required false
        intro: One check before we close.
        right: A negative ratio only says ΔC and ΔE have opposite signs: it fits the dominant southeast and the dominated northwest equally well.
        wrong: Both of those quadrants produce a negative ratio, so the number alone cannot separate them.
        * Southeast: cheaper and better
        * Northwest: costlier and worse
        + Either one — the sign can't tell you

A sign it cannot defend, a denominator it cannot survive, and no place in a ranking — next time we replace it with net monetary benefit, which has none of those faults.
    draw title delivery grave

## Net Benefit Straightens It Out
voice: male
level: advanced
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    wtp_threshold: 275000
    x_label: Incremental effect ΔE (QALYs)
    y_label: Incremental cost ΔC (kr)
    points:
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1.2, cost: 90000}
      - {label: Rehab programme, effect: 0.3, cost: -40000}
      - {label: New drug, effect: 0.9, cost: 400000}
sources:
    - id: stinnett1998
      title: 'Net health benefits: a new framework for the analysis of uncertainty in cost-effectiveness analysis. Medical Decision Making 18(2 Suppl):S68–S80'
      authors: Stinnett AA, Mullahy J
      year: 1998
      finding: Replacing the ICER with net health (or monetary) benefit avoids the statistical problems of ratios and makes options directly comparable at a given threshold.

Now that we have seen how the ICER lies — negative ratios, dominance, extended dominance — let's rewrite the same plane in a number that cannot misbehave that way.
    draw x_axis y_axis

    draw q_ne q_nw q_se q_sw parallel true

A hip replacement: 1.2 QALYs gained for 90 000 kroner more per patient.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

A rehab programme: 0.3 QALYs, and it saves 40 000 kroner — the case whose ICER came out negative and meaningless.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

And a new drug: 0.9 QALYs for 400 000 kroner, an ICER of about 444 000 per QALY.
    draw pt_2 pt_label_2

    pause 0.3

Take the threshold as given for now — 275 000 kroner per QALY, Norway's low-severity level; where that number comes from is next lecture's problem.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

Net monetary benefit converts the health gain into kroner at that price, then subtracts what it cost.
    text formula "NMB = λ·ΔE − ΔC" x 500 y 712 font_size 32

The hip: 275 000 times 1.2 is 330 000 kroner of health, minus 90 000 spent — plus 240 000.
    highlight pt_0 effect glow

The rehab programme: 82 500 of health, plus the 40 000 it saves — plus 122 500, and nothing was divided by anything.
    highlight pt_1 effect glow

The drug: 247 500 of health against 400 000 of cost — minus 152 500.
    highlight pt_2 effect glow

The threshold line is exactly the set of points whose net benefit is zero — so net benefit is the vertical distance from that line, read off in kroner.
    point at.ref wtp_line gesture underline

    quiz "A point sitting ABOVE the threshold line has:" intro "A quick check."
        right: Above the line the extra cost exceeds λ times the health gained, so NMB is negative.
        wrong: Above the line means you paid more than the health is worth at λ — that is a negative net benefit.
        * Positive net benefit
        + Negative net benefit
        * An undefined ICER

Now apply severity: for the most severe conditions the accepted λ rises to 825 000, and the whole line pivots.
    animate wtp_threshold 825000 duration 3

The drug's net benefit turns positive, plus 342 500 — and it now outranks the rehab programme's 287 500, so the ordering itself moved with λ.
    highlight pt_2 pt_1 effect glow

    quiz "So what does net benefit still require of you?"
        right: Net benefit removes the division, not the judgement: λ must be chosen up front, and the ranking depends on it.
        wrong: The pivot just showed it — change λ and the signs and the ranking change with it.
        + A value of λ, committed to before you look at the data
        * Nothing — it is threshold-free
        * Only that ΔE is positive

Strip the picture away, and every option collapses into one signed number — Stinnett and Mullahy's 1998 fix for a statistics problem, since a ratio of two uncertain quantities has no well-behaved confidence interval and a difference does.
    clear

Fix λ at the high-severity level, and each intervention gets a value in kroner.
    text nmb_head "Net monetary benefit at λ = 825 000 kr/QALY" x 500 y 600 font_size 30

They rank straight down the column, and each is comparable with every other — not merely with placebo.
    text nmb_1 "Hip replacement:   + 900 000 kr" #2f6b8f x 500 y 515 font_size 28
    text nmb_2 "New drug:   + 342 500 kr" #2f6b8f x 500 y 455 font_size 28
    text nmb_3 "Rehab programme:   + 287 500 kr" #2f6b8f x 500 y 395 font_size 28
    parallel true

Nusinersen: 1.8 QALYs for roughly nine million kroner per patient — minus 7.5 million, even at the most generous threshold Norway allows.
    text nmb_4 "Nusinersen:   − 7 515 000 kr" #b5482e x 500 y 320 font_size 28

And because these are kroner, they add up — across patients, across programmes, across a whole budget; ICERs never could.
    mark mark target nmb_4 kind box

Net benefit doesn't abolish the hard judgement, it moves it to the front: name the price of a QALY first, and the arithmetic stops fighting you.
    delivery grave

